    reflection: "When you picture a Sunday that genuinely refreshes you—body, mind, and spirit—what does it look like?"

  - title: "The Sabbath as a Tithe of Time"
    scriptures:
      - display: "Ezekiel 20:12"
        url: "ot/ezek/20?id=p12#p12"
      - display: "D&C 59:15–16"
        url: "dc-testament/dc/59?id=p15#p15"
    practice: |
      <h4>One Day in Seven Consecrates the Whole</h4>
      <p>The pattern of tithing is well understood among Latter-day Saints: when we give a tenth of our increase to God, He promises to "open you the windows of heaven, and pour you out a blessing, that there shall not be room enough to receive it" (<a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/ot/mal/3?id=p10#p10" target="_blank">Malachi 3:10</a>). The Sabbath operates on the same principle, but with time rather than treasure. One day in seven, set apart for God, consecrates the remaining six. The part given to God hallows the whole.</p>
      <p>God made this connection explicit when He gave the Sabbath to Israel as more than a rest—it was a sign: "Moreover also I gave them my sabbaths, to be a sign between me and them, that they might know that I am the Lord that sanctify them" (<a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/ot/ezek/20?id=p12#p12" target="_blank">Ezekiel 20:12</a>). The Sabbath was a weekly reminder of who Israel's God was—and equally, of who Israel was. In keeping it, they declared not only that they believed in Him but that they belonged to Him. Latter-day Saints inherit this same sign. When we keep the Sabbath, we are not only resting from labor. We are staking a claim: this day is His, and because this day is His, our lives belong to Him.</p>
      <p>The promise attached to faithful Sabbath observance is remarkable in its breadth: "Inasmuch as ye do this, the fulness of the earth is yours" (<a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/dc-testament/dc/59?id=p16#p16" target="_blank">D&C 59:16</a>). The person who gives one day to God discovers that the other six days carry a quality the seven-day worker never finds.</p>
    blesses: |
      <h4>What the Week Becomes</h4>
      <p>There was a season in my life when I treated the week as seven identical days, all equally available for work, errands, and getting ahead. Sundays had a slightly different texture—a little quieter, maybe a church meeting—but I did not guard the day. I certainly did not set it apart. Looking back, I can see what I could not see then: I was tired in a way that sleep did not fix. The exhaustion was not physical. It was the exhaustion of a life with no hinge, no holy pause, no day that pointed beyond itself.</p>
      <p>We testify, in the name of Jesus Christ, that keeping the Sabbath refreshes the soul and renews the mind. We worship God every day of the week—but we witness that the Lord's Day is a special time of communion with God and brings blessings that come in no other way. The week that begins with God is a different kind of week. Not easier, necessarily—but oriented. Aimed. Lit from the inside in a way that ordinary Sundays are not.</p>
      <p class="bridge-text">The commitment on the next tab is an invitation to try the experiment: give God one day and see what happens to the other six.</p>
    commitments:
      - text: "I will keep the Sabbath as a covenant this week—turning away from worldly pursuits, attending worship, and treating the day as a tithe of my time that belongs wholly to God."
        tier: covenant
      - text: "I will protect a block of time this Sunday from work and distraction, and use it for something that points upward—worship, study, prayer, or service—and observe what effect it has on my week."
        tier: seeker
      - text: "I will try one small Sunday experiment this week: step away from screens for a few hours and spend that time in rest, reflection, or connection with someone I love."
        tier: explore
    reflection: "If the Sabbath is a tithe of your time, what would it mean to give that day without reservation?"
